<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Praeneste</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 338 BCE (approx.) · capital Praeneste</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latin hill city east of Rome, famous for its princely tombs, its bronze cistae and the oracle of Fortuna Primigenia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Praeneste, modern Palestrina, occupied a spur of the Monti Prenestini commanding the routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. In the 7th century BCE its rulers were buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardini-tomb">Bernardini</a> and Barberini tombs with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> from the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, among the richest burials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Praeneste was a powerful and often independent member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latin-league">Latin League</a>. Its workshops produced engraved bronze cistae, cylindrical boxes for toiletries such as the Ficoroni cista. The sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-fortuna-primigenia">Fortuna Primigenia</a>, where oracles were drawn by lot, was later rebuilt on a vast terraced scale in the 2nd century BCE. Local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> had distinct features noted by Roman writers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Praeneste fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a> and lost land in 338 BCE, but remained a separate allied city until it gained citizenship after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>. It sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius-the-younger">Gaius Marius the Younger</a> in 82 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> took it, executed its men and planted a colony.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/praeneste.jpg" alt="Praeneste" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Vonvikken · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_comune_of_Palestrina_(province_of_Rome,_region_Lazio,_Italy).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 338 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Praeneste</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Palestrina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-494&amp;focus=praeneste" aria-label="Praeneste on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4994 688 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Praeneste lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5314 944L5309 947M5314 944L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5309 947h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5314 944h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5314 944h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-494&amp;focus=praeneste">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

